## Thread.Abort Migration → .NET 8

### Why this breaks

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, `ResetAbort()` is gone. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation**.

### Pattern classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `GetNextItem()` blocking | Blocking call interruption | Pass token, use cancellable wait |

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token))
        {
            IsBackground = true
        };
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking can be interrupted
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Equivalent of the old ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we control whether to continue via the loop condition.
                // Loop will exit because token.IsCancellationRequested is true.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful shutdown (replaces Thread.Sleep + Abort)
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key changes explained

1. **`CancellationTokenSource` replaces `Thread.Abort`** — `Cancel()` signals all holders of the token cooperatively.
2. **`OperationCanceledException` replaces `ThreadAbortException`** — the `when` guard ensures you only catch *your* cancellation.
3. **`ResetAbort()` is simply removed** — you control continuation via the loop condition; no runtime re-throw to suppress.
4. **`Thread.Join(timeout)` replaces `Sleep` + `Abort`** — blocks until the thread exits or timeout elapses. No force-kill.
5. **Token must be passed to `GetNextItem`** — if it blocks (e.g., on a `BlockingCollection`), use `.Take(token)` or `WaitHandle.WaitAny` so cancellation can interrupt it. **This is the most critical pitfall** — without this, `Cancel()` won't wake a blocked call.

### Common pitfalls

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| `GetNextItem()` still blocks ignoring token | Use `BlockingCollection<T>.Take(token)` or async I/O with token |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Implement `IDisposable` (shown above) |
| Expecting `Cancel()` to be instant | It only works at the next checkpoint — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
| Swallowing `OperationCanceledException` broadly | Use the `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` guard to avoid catching unrelated cancellations |
